'''''Corynocarpus laevigatus''''', commonly known as '''karaka''' or the '''New Zealand laurel''', is an [[evergreen]] tree in the family [[Corynocarpaceae]]. It is [[endemic (ecology)|endemic]] to New Zealand and is common throughout the [[North Island]] and less common in the [[South Island]]. ''C. laevigatus'' individuals are also found on the [[Chatham Islands]], [[Kermadec Islands]], and the [[Three Kings Islands]]. ''C. laevigatus'' is mainly a coastal tree, although in the North Island, it is also found inland.

''Corynocarpus laevigatus'' was first [[Species description|described]] in 1776 by the German naturalists [[Georg Forster|Georg]] and [[Johann Reinhold Forster]]. ''C. laevigatus'' grows to heights of up to {{convert|15–20|m|abbr=off|sigfig=1}} and has a [[Trunk (botany)|trunk]] of up to {{convert|60|cm|abbr=off|sigfig=1}} in diameter. Its leaves are leathery, dark to bright green in colour and up to {{convert|15–30|cm|abbr=on|sigfig=1}} long. ''C. laevigatus'' produces large oval-shaped orange-coloured fruits. ''C. laevigatus'' seeds are highly toxic to humans and contain poisonous [[toxin]]s and other [[glucoside]]s. The fruits are a valuable food source for the [[kererū]] and the [[Chatham Islands pigeon]]. ''C. laevigatus'' has been introduced to the United States for reforestation purposes; it is [[naturalisation (biology)|naturalised]] and considered an [[invasive species]] on several [[Hawaii]]an islands and is mainly found on the island of [[Kauai]].

It is considered a {{lang|mi|[[taonga]]}} (cultural treasure) amongst the [[Māori people|Māori]] and [[Moriori people]]s, who valued ''C. laevigatus'' for its [[drupe]]s and [[seed]]s. On the Chatham Islands, depictions of Moriori ancestors were carved on to ''C. laevigatus'' trees (known as {{lang|rrm|rākau momori}}) and are considered internationally significant and unique to their culture. An [[exoplanet]] originally named [[HD 137388]] was renamed to "Karaka" in 2019 in recognition of the tree's orange-coloured fruit.

==Description==

''Corynocarpus laevigatus'' (karaka) is a medium-sized [[evergreen]] leafy [[Canopy (biology)|canopy]] tree with erect spreading branches. It is [[endemism|endemic]] to New Zealand. It grows to heights of up to {{convert|15–20|m|abbr=off}} and has a stout [[Trunk (botany)|trunk]] usually up to {{convert|60|cm|abbr=off}} in diameter, but can be up to {{convert|1|m|abbr=off}} in diameter. The largest trunk of a ''C. laevigatus'' specimen diameter ever measured was {{convert|3|m|abbr=on|0}} in diameter.{{Sfnm|Rapson|2012|Kirk|1889|2p=171}}{{sfn|McAlpine|Cory|Cox|McLeod (Taranaki Whānui)|2024|p=3}} The [[bark (botany)|bark]] is typically a grey colour, although on young trees it can be a lighter brown-colour.{{Sfn|De Lange|2012}}{{Sfn|Rapson|2012}} ''C. laevigatus'' is predominantly a coastal tree, although in the [[North Island]], it can also be found in lowland inland forests.{{sfnm|Metcalf|2000|1p=145|Cheeseman|1906|2p=105}}{{Sfnm|Kirk|1889|1p=173|Metcalf|2002|2p=98}} ''C. laevigatus'' is suggested to have a maximum lifespan of 500 years in mainland New Zealand, and potentially 600 years from a specimen found on [[Hen_and_Chicken_Islands#Lady_Alice_Island|Lady Alice Island]].{{sfn|Costall|Carter|Shimada|Anthony|2006|p=12}}

Mature trees have dark-brown corrugated bark with corrugations that are broken up into pieces that are roughly {{convert|1x3|cm|abbr=on|1}} and {{convert|0.5|cm|abbr=on|1}} thick. Younger trees have light brown bark that frequently has short, horizontal bands that resemble sewing stitches. ''C. laevigatus'' has alternating elliptic oval-shaped [[leaves]] that are up to {{convert|8|cm|abbr=on|0}} wide, with [[Petiole (botany)|petiole]]s less than {{convert|2|cm|abbr=on|1}} long.{{Sfn|Rapson|2012}}

From August to November, ''C. laevigatus'' produces large, stout, erect [[panicle]]s of tiny greenish-yellow flowers, each less than {{convert|0.5|cm|abbr=on|1}} in diameter. It starts [[Flower#Transition|flowering]] between August and November, and each panicle may have up to 100–200 flowers.{{Sfnm|Rapson|2012|Kirk|1889|2p=171}}{{sfn|McAlpine|Cory|Cox|McLeod (Taranaki Whānui)|2024|p=3}} Its [[sepal]]s are rounded, and its [[petal]]s are vaguely [[wikt:spathulate|spathulate]] (spoon-shaped).{{Sfn|De Lange|2012}} Each panicle on an [[inflorescence]] (flower cluster) can be up to 200&nbsp;mm long.{{Sfn|De Lange|2012}} The fruit ''C. laevigatus'' produces are oval-shaped and {{convert|30–40|mm|abbr=on}} in length; with pale yellow–orange coloured [[flesh]] and a poisonous [[seed]] which is smooth and elliptic. The seed has an open system of [[fiber|fibrous]] veins on the yellowish surface.{{sfn|Poole|Adams|1963|p=128}}{{Sfnm|Rapson|2012|De Lange|2012|Metcalf|2002|3p=98}} The fruit has a sickly sweet taste, reminiscent of [[apricot]]s or [[Date palm|date]]s.{{Sfn|Vennell|2019|pp=70–75}} Its leaves are dark green in colour, paler green beneath, thick, leathery, and are vaguely [[wikt:obovate|obovate]] (egg-shaped) to [[wikt:oblong|oblong]] (rectangular) in character.{{Sfn|De Lange|2012}}

''Corynocarpus laevigatus''{{'}}s [[wood anatomy]] prevents them from being dated using the conventional technique of counting annual [[growth ring]]s in the trunk. ''C. laevigatus'' specimens planted in [[Palmerston North]] in 1962 grew to a height of {{convert|14|m|abbr=on}} in 42 years. The largest stem diameter measured {{convert|26.5|cm|abbr=on}}, and the trees grew {{convert|0.68|mm|abbr=on}} per year. Other ''C. laevigatus'' trees grew at different rates; the largest ''C. laevigatus'' tree in mainland New Zealand was 500 years old with a [[diameter at breast height]] (DBH) of {{convert|212|cm|abbr=on}} and a growth rate of {{convert|0.43|cm|abbr=on}} per year.{{Sfn|Rapson|2012}} The fruits of ''C. laevigatus'' usually [[Ripening|ripen]] between January and April and the [[seed]]s are mainly dispersed by two native [[columbiform]] birds.{{Sfnm|De Lange|2012|Rapson|2012}}{{sfn|McAlpine|Cory|Cox|McLeod (Taranaki Whānui)|2024|p=3}} ''C. laevigatus'' has a [[Ploidy|diploid]] chromosome count of 46.{{sfn|Dawson|1997|p=1}}

===Phytochemistry===
At least 11 [[chemical compound|compounds]] have been identified in ''C. laevigatus''. A unique nitropropanoyl [[glucopyranose]] called 1,4,6-tri-(3-nitropropanoyl)-β-D-glucopyranose (corynocarpin) was first identified and extracted by a 1978 study published in ''[[Phytochemistry (journal)|Phytochemistry]]''.{{sfn|Moyer|Pfeffer|Valentine|Gustine|1979|p=1}} A 2025 study examined the phytochemical screening of ''C. laevigatus'' extracts and found that the tree contains "significant amounts of phytochemicals".{{sfn|Obike|Eze|Abdel-Rahman|Ikeuba|2025|p=14}}

