''Corynocarpus laevigatus'' was first [[Species description|described]] by the German naturalists [[Johann Reinhold Forster]] and [[Georg Forster]] in 1776. Despite this, their description was incomplete, and the figures of the flowers were inaccurate. The species was first described from specimens collected by Johann Reinhold Forster and Georg Forster on the [[second voyage of James Cook]]. [[Joseph Banks]] and [[Daniel Solander]], who were the botanists on the [[first voyage of James Cook]], brought specimens of ''C. laevigatus'' to England, where they described and recorded it under the name ''Merretia lucida'', although their work was never formally published.{{sfn|Hemsley|1903}}

''Corynocarpus laevigatus'' is the only member of the family [[Corynocarpaceae]] found in New Zealand.{{Sfn|Vennell|2019|pp=70–75}}  The four other species in the [[genus]] grow across the Pacific Islands and Australia, including: (''[[Corynocarpus similis|C. similis]]'') in Vanuatu, (''[[Corynocarpus cribbianus|C. cribbianus]]'') in the Solomon Islands, North Queensland and Vanuatu; (''[[Corynocarpus dissimilis|C. dissimilis]]'') in New Caledonia; and (''[[Corynocarpus rupestris|C. rupestris]]'') in the Australian states of [[New South Wales]] and [[Queensland]].{{Sfn|Rapson|2012}}

The ''[[Corynocarpus]]'' genus may have originated from a [[Paleotropical]] centre, then separated into two radiations into colder climates. In the first separation, ''C. cribbianus'' and ''C. rupestris'' were found in Australia through New Guinea, while in the second separation, ''C. dissimilis'', ''C. similis'', and ''C. laevigatus'' were found in New Caledonia, which led to New Zealand. Fossilised kernels from the genus ''Corynocarpus'', from the early [[Miocene]] era, were found at [[Landslip Hill]] in the [[Southland Region]]. This discovery indicates that the genus has a long history in New Zealand. It is possible that the ''Corynocarpus'' genus spread from New Caledonia to New Zealand via land connections that existed in the mid-[[Tertiary period|Tertiary]] era.{{sfn|Atherton|Lockhart|McLenachan|de Lange|2015|pp=1–2}}

===Etymology===
The [[etymology]] (word origin) of the genus name ''Corynocarpus'' derives from the [[Greek language|Greek]] {{lang|el|koryne}}, meaning 'club', and {{lang|el|carpus}}, meaning 'fruit', translating in English to 'club fruit'. The [[specific epithet]] (second part of the scientific name) originates from the [[Latin]] {{lang|la|laevigatus}} meaning 'smooth', in reference either to the fruit, the leaves or the skin.{{Sfn|De Lange|2012}}{{sfn|McAlpine|Cory|Cox|McLeod (Taranaki Whānui)|2024|p=3}} The species is [[common name|commonly known]] as karaka or the New Zealand laurel.{{Sfn|De Lange|2012}}

In the [[Māori language]], karaka can either refer to the fruit of the tree or the tree itself.{{Sfn|Te Aka Māori Dictionary}} The word has origins in [[Proto-Polynesian language]]s. [[Cognate]]s of karaka are used in [[Polynesian languages]] for members of the ''[[Planchonella]]'' genus, which share a similar appearance with ''Corynocarpus laevigatus''.{{Sfn|Vennell|2019|pp=70–75}} Karaka is also the Māori word for the colour orange,{{sfn|McAlpine|Cory|Cox|McLeod (Taranaki Whānui)|2024|p=23}}{{sfn|Poole|Adams|1963|p=128}} and has likely only been in use since the 19th century, as no word meaning orange appears in early Māori language dictionaries.{{sfn|Dodgson|Chen|Zahido|2024|p=16}} In the [[Moriori language]] and on the [[Chatham Islands]], both the tree and the fruit are known as {{lang|rrm|kōpi}}. This name may share an etymology with Polynesian names used to describe members of the true ginger genus, ''[[Zingiber]]''.{{Sfn|Vennell|2019|pp=70–75}} In English, ''C. laevigatus'' is also commonly known as the "New Zealand laurel".{{sfn|Garnock-Jones|Brockie|FitzJohn|2007|p=6}}

==Distribution==
''Corynocarpus laevigatus'' is found in large numbers throughout the North Island and [[South Island]] as far south as the [[Banks Peninsula]] on the east coast of the South Island and [[Greymouth]], on the [[West Coast Region|West Coast]].{{sfnm|Metcalf|2000|1p=145|Cheeseman|1906|2p=105}}{{Sfnm|Kirk|1889|1p=173|Metcalf|2002|2p=98}} It is also found on the Chatham Islands, [[Kermadec Islands]], and the [[Three Kings Islands]].{{sfn|Atherton|Lockhart|McLenachan|de Lange|2015|pp=1–2}} It is possible that before Polynesian arrival in New Zealand, ''C. laevigatus'' was likely restricted to the far north of the North Island, despite its current range across offshore islands and the northern half of the South Island, which likely spread from Māori plantings.{{sfn|McAlpine|Cory|Cox|McLeod (Taranaki Whānui)|2024|p=1}}{{sfn|Maxwell|Tromp|2016|p=2}} Populations are often found in association with former [[pā]] (Māori village) sites.{{Sfn|Vennell|2019|pp=70–75}}{{Sfn|Mirza|2005|p=349}}

The species was not naturally present in the [[Otago]] and [[Southland Region]]s of New Zealand prior to human settlement. All individuals south of the Banks Peninsula are likely to be recent introductions. The distribution in Otago and Southland is naturally uncommon and scattered along the coastline.{{sfn|Atherton|Lockhart|McLenachan|de Lange|2015|p=2}}{{sfn|McAlpine|Cory|Cox|McLeod (Taranaki Whānui)|2024|pp=5–7}} ''C. laevigatus''{{'}}s South Island population is exclusively located near coastal areas and most of the population is centred in the Banks Peninsula (including [[Christchurch]]'s coastal areas) and the [[Marlborough Sounds]].{{sfn|Atherton|Lockhart|McLenachan|de Lange|2015|p=2}} ''C. laevigatus'' has been [[Introduced species|introduced]] to the United States, it is naturalised and considered an [[invasive species]] in [[Hawaii]], where it is naturalised in wet soils and considered a threat to endangered or rare species.{{Sfn|Sawyer|McFadgen|Hughes|2003|p=10}}{{sfn|Wagner|Herbst|Sohmer|1999|p=1952}} ''C. laevigatus'' was introduced to Hawaii for [[reforestation]] purposes and was first naturalised to [[Kauai]] in 1891, and is still commonly found throughout the island.{{Sfn|Little|Skolmen|1989|p=176}}{{sfn|Wagner|Herbst|Sohmer|1999|p=1952}} ''C. laevigatus'' is primarily naturalised on the island of Kauai, it is also found on the islands of [[Hawaii (island)|Hawaii]], [[Molokai]], and [[Oahu]].{{Sfn|Gallaher|Brock|Kennedy|Imada|2020}} ''C. laevigatus'' is also found in [[Southern California]].{{Sfn|Little|Skolmen|1989|p=176}}{{Sfn|Degener|Degener|1965|pp=206–206}}

==Ecology==
[[File:Kererū eating karaka berries.jpg|230px|thumb|right|''C. laevigatus''{{'}}s fruits are a valuable food source for the [[kererū]]|alt=A kererū, a purple greenish white coloured bird, sitting in a fruiting karaka tree, surrounded by orange coloured fruits.]]
The fruits that ''C. laevigatus'' produces are a valuable food source for native New Zealand birds.{{Sfn|Sawyer|McFadgen|Hughes|2003|p=7}} The [[kererū]] (''Hemiphaga novaeseelandiae'') and the [[Chatham Islands pigeon]] (''Hemiphaga chathamensis'') are the only native bird species with a [[gape]] large enough to consume the fruits of ''C. laevigatus''.{{sfn|McAlpine|Cory|Cox|McLeod (Taranaki Whānui)|2024|p=5}}

The [[New Zealand bellbird]]s (''Anthornis melanura'') have been observed feeding on the [[sap]] from the bark of ''C. laevigatus''.{{Sfn|Rapson|2012}} Other smaller native birds, such as the [[North Island robin]] (''Petroica longipes''), [[silvereye]] (''Zosterops lateralis'') and [[Whitehead (bird)|whitehead]]s (''Mohoua albicilla'') are known to search around ''C. laevigatus'' for insects.{{sfn|McAlpine|Cory|Cox|McLeod (Taranaki Whānui)|2024|p=13}}

The [[endocarp]]s of ''C. laevigatus'' are sometimes nibbled by [[Polynesian rat]] (''Rattus exulans''), while the larger [[brown rat]]s (''Rattus norvegicus'') sometimes chew through the endocarps. Both species consume the flesh of ''C. laevigatus''.{{Sfn|Rapson|2012}} [[Possums in New Zealand|Possums]], an invasive species in New Zealand, consume the flesh of ''C. laevigatus'' fruits.{{Sfn|Kerridge|2018}} The extinct [[moa]] and other large birds likely consumed the fruits of ''C. laevigatus'' centuries ago.{{sfn|Clout|Karl|Pierce|Robertson|1995|pp=264–271}} ''C. laevigatus'' plays host to the endemic New Zealand beetle  species ''[[Oemona hirta]]''.{{Sfn|Sopow|Bain|2017|pp=12–13}} ''C. laevigatus'' is one of New Zealand's least flammable tree species with the highest moisture contents and lowest flammability rates, as examined in a 2016 study.{{sfn|Wyse|Perry|O'Connell|Holland|2016|pp=6, 9}}

==Relationship with humans==
===Cultivation===
''Corynocarpus laevigatus'' is common in cultivation and widely available for sale both in New Zealand and elsewhere in the world.{{Sfn|De Lange|2012}} It was one of the most grown food crops by pre-European Māori (alongside [[Sweet potato cultivation in Polynesia|kūmara]] and [[aruhe]]); they ate the [[drupe]] and seed after a long [[detoxification]] process.{{Sfn|Sawyer|McFadgen|Hughes|2003}}{{sfnm|Metcalf|2000|1p=145|Reed|1963|2p=49|Colenso|1880}} ''C. laevigatus''{{'}}s seeds contain highly toxic [[glucosides]] of [[3-nitropropionic acid]], such as [[karakin]].{{sfn|McAlpine|Cory|Cox|McLeod (Taranaki Whānui)|2024|p=9}}{{Sfnm|Saggers|2017|Skey|1871}}{{sfn|Majak|Benn|1994|p=901}} Some initial symptoms of poisoning include [[diarrhoea]], [[nausea]] and restlessness which develop to more severe gastrointestinal and neurological problems.{{Sfn|Willis|2020}}

Every autumn, pre-colonisation Māori could collect the seeds dropped from ''C. laevigatus'' trees. The seeds could be placed in open-weaved [[Kete (basket)|traditional baskets]] ({{lang|mi|kete}}), washed in water to remove the outer pulp and baked and sun dried, a process that would remove toxicity from the seeds.{{Sfnm|Colenso|1880|Reed|1963|2p=100}}{{Sfn|Laing|1906|pp=234–235}}

===In Māori culture===
[[File:Kopi tree carving, Canterbury Museum, 2016-01-27-2.jpg|160px|thumb|right|A Moriori {{lang|rrm|kōpi}} tree carving ({{lang|rrm|rākau momori}}) in the [[Canterbury Museum, Christchurch|Canterbury Museum]]|alt=A wooden plank displaying an artistic carving of a human-like figure, representing an ancestor of the Moriori people.]]

''Corynocarpus laevigatus'' is of great importance to Māori, who primarily used it as a food source.{{sfn|Costall|Carter|Shimada|Anthony|2006|pp=6–7}}{{sfn|Metcalf|2002|p=98}} In [[Māori mythology]], ''C. laevigatus'' is told to be from [[Hawaiki]], an ancestral homeland for the Māori people.{{sfn|Costall|Carter|Shimada|Anthony|2006|pp=6–7}}{{Sfn|Laing|1906|p=235}} While pre-European Māori primarily valued ''C. laevigatus'' for its nutrition rather than medicine, they did use leaf undersides to draw out [[infection]]s and fresh upper surfaces to heal injured skin.{{sfn|McAlpine|Cory|Cox|McLeod (Taranaki Whānui)|2024|p=10}} The seeds of ''C. laevigatus'' were of great value to Māori and needed to be prepared before they could be safely consumed, while the flesh of the fruits was consumed uncooked. The seeds are very poisonous and bitter in taste which had to be steamed in earth ovens ({{lang|mi|[[hāngī|umu]]}}).{{sfnm|Metcalf|2000|1p=145|Cheeseman|1906|2p=105}}{{Sfnm|Reed|1963|p=142|Kirk|1889|2p=172|Metcalf|2002|3p=98}} ''C. laevigatus''{{'}}s timber was also used in constructing canoes ({{lang|mi|[[waka (canoe)|waka]]}}).{{sfn|McAlpine|Cory|Cox|McLeod (Taranaki Whānui)|2024|p=12}}

===In Moriori culture===
{{See also|Hāpūpū / J M Barker Historic Reserve}}
On the Chatham Islands, ''C. laevigatus'' have played a distinguished role in the history of the indigenous [[Moriori people]]s; the bark of the ''C. laevigatus'' trees has been notably used for carving [[dendroglyph]]s rather than a food source.{{sfn|Maxwell|2017|pp=1–2}} A 2000 [[Department of Conservation (New Zealand)|Department of Conservation]] report documented 147 ''C. laevigatus'' specimens with dendroglyphs on the Chatham Islands, although not all carvings were confirmed as authentically Moriori.{{sfn|Jopson|McKibbin|2000|p=8}}{{sfn|Atherton|Lockhart|McLenachan|de Lange|2015|p=12}}

''Corynocarpus laevigatus'' (or {{lang|rrm|kōpi}} in Moriori) is considered a {{lang|mi|[[taonga]]}} (cultural treasure) amongst the Māori and Moriori peoples. Moriori also utilised ''C. laevigatus'' trees by carving on them; known as {{lang|rrm|rākau momori}} in the Moriori language, the carvings typically depict Moriori [[ancestor]]s and are considered internationally significant and unique to their culture. A {{lang|mi|[[rāhui]]}} protects the remaining carved trees due to their fragile state. ''C. laevigatus'' timber was also utilised by the Moriori to smoke and preserve food.{{sfn|McAlpine|Cory|Cox|McLeod (Taranaki Whānui)|2024|pp=1–2, 12}}{{sfn|Jopson|McKibbin|2000|pp=5–7}}

===Poisonings===

''Corynocarpus laevigatus'' has been linked to dog poisonings in New Zealand, leading to calls for the plant to be removed from urban areas.{{Sfn|Vennell|2019|pp=70–75}}{{sfn|McAlpine|Cory|Cox|McLeod (Taranaki Whānui)|2024}}

===Recognition===
An [[exoplanet]] originally named [[HD 137388]] was renamed to "Karaka" in 2019 in honour of the tree's orange fruit.{{Sfn|Betson|2019|p=1}} A small community {{convert|20|km|abbr=on}} west of [[Whanganui]] named [[Pākaraka]] is named in honour of the tree and its name reflects the "abundance of karaka trees" that previously were situated there.{{Sfn|Tahana|2022}} [[Karaka Bay (Auckland)|Karaka Bay]], east of Auckland Central, in the suburb of Glendowie, is also named in honour of the tree. There are three other bays in New Zealand that are named "Karaka". Additionally, [[Karaka, New Zealand|a locality in South Auckland]], approximately {{convert|40|km|abbr=on}} from [[Auckland CBD|Auckland Central]] is also named in honour of the tree.{{Sfn|Mirza|2005|p=349}} [[New Zealand Post]] recognised ''C. laevigatus'' in 1960 by featuring it on penny [[postage stamp]]s.{{sfn|McAlpine|Cory|Cox|McLeod (Taranaki Whānui)|2024|p=14}}{{Sfn|NZ Post|2025}}

